+# Single-Node Dev on k3d
+
+[← Docs Index](../README.md)
+
+Run the platform on one machine inside [k3d](https://k3d.io) (k3s in Docker) for development, demos and CI. This is the [bring-your-own cluster](topologies.md#bring-your-own-kubernetes-byo) path: k3d creates the cluster, and the installer deploys the platform and the layers above it onto that cluster. It is a development topology, not a production one.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> The settings on this page need `istio_platform` in the istio role and the control-plane affinity in the envoy_gateway role. Without them, keep `istio_enabled: false`, and expect the Envoy DaemonSet to schedule no pods on k3s (it selects `node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: ""`, k3s sets `"true"`).
+
+---
+
+## What differs on k3d
+
+| Concern | On k3d / k3s | Setting |
+|---|---|---|
+| Cluster provisioning | k3d creates the cluster; Kubespray is skipped | `existing_kubernetes: <kubeconfig>` |
+| `LoadBalancer` Services | k3s ServiceLB answers them on the node | `metallb_enabled: false` |
+| Storage | k3s ships the `local-path` provisioner as the default StorageClass | `storage_backend: local-path` (the default) |
+| Istio CNI paths | CNI config lives in `/var/lib/rancher/k3s/agent/etc/cni/net.d`, containerd loads CNI binaries from `/var/lib/rancher/k3s/data/cni` (its `bin_dirs`), not `/bin` | `istio_platform: k3s` (the `k3d` profile installs `istio-cni` into `/bin`, where containerd does not look) |
+| Ingress controller | k3s bundles Traefik, which would claim ports 80 and 443 | `--disable=traefik` at cluster create |
+| CPU pinning | containerd NRI is on unless k3s runs in a user namespace (rootless) | default `nri-balloons`; `best-effort` if NRI is off or another NRI policy already owns the node |
+| Control-plane label | k3s labels servers `node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane=true` | none |
+
+The hardware minimums in [Prerequisites](../quickstart/prerequisites.md#hardware-minimums) apply to the host. k3d sets no CPU or memory limit on its node containers unless asked to.
+
+---
+
+## Step 1 — Create the cluster
+
+Requires Docker and k3d v5. One server and no agents gives a single-node cluster, so node topology labels are a no-op.
+
+```bash
+k3d cluster create eas \
+  --image rancher/k3s:v1.34.12-k3s1 \
+  --k3s-arg '--disable=traefik@server:*' \
+  -p '80:80@loadbalancer' \
+  -p '443:443@loadbalancer' \
+  --wait
+
+mkdir -p ~/.kube
+k3d kubeconfig get eas > ~/.kube/k3d-eas.yaml
+chmod 600 ~/.kube/k3d-eas.yaml
+export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/k3d-eas.yaml   # kubectl and model-manager; a BYO install writes no env/local/kubeconfig.yaml
+kubectl get nodes
+```
+
+The `-p` mappings forward ports 80 and 443 on the host, through k3d's load balancer container, to the ServiceLB pods on the node. The gateway is then reachable on `127.0.0.1`.
+
+---
+
+## Step 2 — Create and configure the environment
+
+```bash
+./es_auto_installer.sh configure
+./es_auto_installer.sh init inference
+```
+
+In `env/local/global_config.yaml`:
+
+```yaml
+existing_kubernetes: "/home/<you>/.kube/k3d-eas.yaml"   # absolute path to the kubeconfig from Step 1
+metallb_enabled: false        # k3s ServiceLB serves LoadBalancer Services
+istio_platform: k3s           # istio-cni uses k3s's CNI directories
+storage_backend: local-path   # provided by k3s
+# kubernetes_cpu_policy: best-effort   # only when NRI is off or another NRI policy owns the node
+```
+
+Only one NRI resource policy can run on a node. If the k3d node already runs one (for example a balloons or topology-aware policy installed with the cluster), set `kubernetes_cpu_policy: best-effort` so the installer does not install a second.
+
+---
+
+## Step 3 — Install
+
+```bash
+./es_auto_installer.sh install inference --env local
+```
+
+---
+
+## Step 4 — Reach the services
+
+ServiceLB reports the node's Docker network address as the gateway IP. With the port mappings from Step 1, point the hostnames at the host's loopback instead:
+
+```bash
+DOMAIN=$(yq '.base_domain_name' env/local/global_config.yaml)
+echo "127.0.0.1  ${DOMAIN} grafana.${DOMAIN} keycloak.${DOMAIN} inference.${DOMAIN} litellm.${DOMAIN}" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
+```
+
+Import the self-signed CA as described in [Getting Started](../quickstart/quickstart.md#access-the-services).
+
+---
+
+## Teardown
+
+```bash
+./es_auto_installer.sh teardown infrastructure --env local
+k3d cluster delete eas
+```
+
+On a BYO cluster, `teardown infrastructure` removes the layers the installer deployed and the node labels it added, and never resets the cluster itself. `k3d cluster delete` removes the cluster.
+
+---
+
+## Limits
+
+- One node only: `local-path` volumes are bound to that node. For more than one node, follow [Multi-Node & BYO Cluster](topologies.md).
+- Rootless Docker or Podman: k3s turns NRI off in a user namespace, so use `kubernetes_cpu_policy: best-effort`.
+- Inference runs on the host CPU through the k3d node; throughput matches the host, not a Xeon server.
+
